  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA pleasant start to the weekend is in store with high pressure\nover the area. Highs today reach the lower 70s to lower 80s. A\nmostly clear sky and light to calm winds will allow for\ntemperatures tonight to once again fall into the 40s and 50s.\nPatchy fog is possible once again in some valley areas.\n\nSunday will start out dry as well, but clouds will increase\nthroughout the day ahead of a progressive upper shortwave and \nsurface low pressure system approaching from the west. This \nsystem will bring widespread rain showers along with some \nrumbles of thunder with some elevated instability present. \nTiming of precipitation looks to be Sunday afternoon into the \nearly evening and continuing overnight. Despite high \nprecipitable water values (1.50 to 2.00 inches), the progressive\nnature of this system and lack of instability should keep any \nconvective elements more isolated in coverage. While most areas \ncould receive as much as 0.50 and 1.00 inches of rain, some \nhigher amounts (likely up to 2 inches) can occur where any \nconvective elements occur. Regardless, some ponding of water on \nroads and areas of poor drainage are possible along with \nisolated instances of flash flooding, especially for any areas \nthat see repeated convective, heavier showers. The latest WPC \noutlook has all of eastern New York and western New England in a\nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. A few \nshowers and thunderstorms will linger into Monday before the \nsystem fully departs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMuch of next week will feature dry and pleasant weather across\neastern New York and western New England. The main weather\nfeature for next week (after Monday) is an approaching cold\nfront which could bring a few showers and thunderstorms late \nWednesday into Thursday. Moisture, initially, with this system \nlooks minimal so overall shower coverage may be more isolated to\nscattered. The timing of this system will likely prevent severe\nweather concerns as well. High temperatures for the early to \nmiddle part of next week will be in the mid-70s to mid-80s \nbefore falling back into the upper 60s to upper 70s by the end \nof the week.",
